    business: "Valterra Platinum is one of the world's leading integrated producers of platinum-group metals (PGMs), demerged from Anglo American in 2025 and primary-listed on the JSE with a secondary listing in London. It owns the full value chain — mining, concentrating, smelting, refining and marketing — across five mining operations (Mogalakwena, Amandelbult, Mototolo and Modikwa in South Africa's Bushveld Complex, and Unki on Zimbabwe's Great Dyke), feeding its own processing assets and selling a five-metal basket — platinum, palladium, rhodium and minor PGMs, plus nickel, copper and chrome co-products — into global markets through hubs in London, Singapore and Shanghai. It is a price-taker on internationally quoted PGM prices: its economics are driven by where its assets sit on the industry cost curve, not by any pricing power of its own.",
    fundamentals: "It sits at the low end of the industry cost curve, and that is the core of the case. As a price-taker with no control over the PGM basket, a producer's most durable, valuation-independent advantage is its relative cost position — and Valterra's is strong. Group all-in sustaining costs were around US$996 per 3E ounce in H1 2026, with the flagship Mogalakwena open pit firmly in the industry's first quartile (~US$985) and Unki lower still; management targets the lower half of the cost curve through the cycle. Low-cost assets keep earning margin at prices that push higher-cost competitors into losses.\nThe moat is geological and integrated, and hard to replicate. PGM orebodies of scale exist almost only in the Bushveld and the Great Dyke; combined with multi-decade reserves (148 million 4E ounces of Ore Reserves and 488 million ounces of Mineral Resources) and captive smelting and refining, this is a barrier a well-funded entrant cannot cross quickly. Vertical integration also captures margin along the chain — own-mined production earns a materially higher margin than purchased or toll-refined metal.\nThrough the cycle it earns returns above its cost of capital. Return on capital has averaged well above a plausible rand cost of capital across two decades, even allowing for genuine loss-making troughs — the mark of a business that creates value over a full cycle rather than only at the top. Its earnings are, however, highly cyclical and price-driven: that is a fundamental characteristic of the business, not a timing point, so the case must be judged on mid-cycle economics rather than any single strong or weak half-year.\nIt is strongly cash-generative and allocates capital with discipline. The balance sheet has been net cash every year since 2018 (R23.7bn at mid-2026), growth is self-funded, and capital is returned through a base dividend (40% of headline earnings) plus a variable/special layer that distributes excess cash at the top of the cycle without gearing the balance sheet — and flexes down first when conditions weaken, protecting sustaining investment and the cost position. There is no history of empire-building M&A; the portfolio has instead been actively high-graded by exiting higher-cost assets over two decades.\nGovernance and incentives are broadly supportive — with watch-items. The board is substantially independent and King IV-compliant, disclosure quality is high, and long-term incentives are weighted toward total shareholder return, return on capital and cash flow. The principal watch-items are the short standalone track record (the company has been independent only since mid-2025 and is untested through a full independent down-cycle), a concentrated state-linked shareholder (the Public Investment Corporation at ~20%), and a safety record that has deteriorated over the past year.",
    risks: "Because the case rests on the durability of a low-cost, long-life franchise, the risks we track are those capable of eroding it. The structural one is demand substitution: battery-electric vehicles gradually shrink autocatalyst demand — roughly two-thirds of platinum, palladium and rhodium demand — a long-dated threat, partly offset by growing industrial and hydrogen uses, but a genuine cap on the addressable market over time. The most immediate operational risk is safety: three fatalities in the first half of 2026 and a rising injury-frequency rate, which carries the risk of regulatory production stoppages. Company-specific, we watch grade decline at Mogalakwena — its head grade has fallen steadily since 2021 — which pressures the first-quartile cost position unless the higher-grade Sandsloot underground project delivers. To these we add the inherent extreme cyclicality of a price-taking commodity producer, South African and Zimbabwean jurisdictional risk (safety regulation, and the export-retention regime that ties up part of Unki's cash), and the short independent track record. None is acute today, but each is a genuine, monitorable threat to the durability of returns — and each matters more when valuation is not there to provide a cushion.",
    portfolioContext: "On its fundamentals, Valterra presents as a best-in-class operator within a cyclical industry — a low-cost, integrated, cash-generative PGM producer with a durable geological cost moat and disciplined, self-funded capital allocation. That quality is the basis on which it earns a place in the opportunity set. It is, by nature, a rand-hedge, PGM-cycle exposure (rand costs, dollar-linked revenue), so at the portfolio level it tends to concentrate rather than diversify existing South African resources and currency exposure across the book. How much to hold, and when — weighed against where the PGM price cycle sits and the valuation on offer — is a separate judgement for the portfolio manager; this tracker is deliberately silent on that so the fundamental case can be assessed on its own merits and combined with the manager's valuation and positioning view rather than pre-empting it.",
