    commentIndustry: "Hybrid structure: a mature, defensive SA core funding structurally growing Pan-Africa (27 countries, insurance penetration <3% of GDP) and India (Shriram). Largest SA non-bank financial institution (~R209bn, ~1.3-4x the nearest listed peers; ~24% SA life share). Regulatory power is the dominant Porter force (India GST/SSV, Malaysia premium cap already repricing product economics). Blended revenue CAGR ~10-14%.",
    commentMoat: "Multi-source moat: scale, capital/solvency licensing barriers (economic solvency 183%), a composite micro-insurance licence, and structurally hard-to-replicate Allianz (Pan-Africa) and Shriram (India) partnerships. Santam's underwriting margin (11.3%) leads its own target, but life VNB margin sits below Discovery's - scale not yet fully converted into best-in-class new-business economics. India/Malaysia regulatory repricing is the single most credible moat threat (cannot out-invest a regulator). Brand equity reinforced (client satisfaction 88%, Santam NPS 57->68).",
    commentManagement: "Adjusted RoGEV/share 15.7% cleared the 14.7% hurdle; RoE 17.1% tracking toward the >20% target; R21.4bn capital generated deployed at a ~20% IRR hurdle. 2026 LTI strengthened (RoE weighting raised to 40%). M&A disciplined (Shriram stake-building) but not costless - Assupol financing + Pan-Africa integration drove a 7% net operational earnings decline in 2025. Prunes sub-scale positions (exited Zimbabwe/Niger/Zambia general insurance and UK asset management in 2025).",
    commentSustainability: "Strong validation: MSCI AAA, B-BBEE Level 1, CDP B, Sustainalytics 'low risk' 16.7 (ISS C- the one below-par benchmark, flagged transparently). High disclosure reliability (AFS jointly assured by KPMG and PwC; SASB/TCFD/TNFD). Climate risk rated inherent-high/residual-medium (Santam geocoding 86% of underwriting). Minor open gaps: B-BBEE-contingent revenue and own energy-security position unquantified (low materiality for a financial-services model).",
    commentMacro: "Overlay: NEUTRAL, tilting mild TAILWIND. Net rand quantified - ~71-76% SA, ~24% offshore -> a weak rand is a net tailwind (2025 earnings fell 7% specifically on H2 rand strength). SA CPI 5.0% (oil-shock-linked) with SARB restrictive at 7% weighs on near-term new-business volumes; Moody's positive sovereign outlook supports a lower country risk premium. The SARB's 23 Sept 2026 decision is the key swing event.",
